  "step1_summary": "* Chief complaint: Severe headaches and feeling hot, with additional systemic symptoms (tachycardia, shortness of breath, weight loss, reduced appetite, tingling in arms, decreased libido) that occur intermittently, mainly at work.\n* Symptoms (onset/duration/severity/aggravating factors):\n  - Onset roughly after changing jobs; episodes come and go, occur mostly while at work (meetings, laptop use, computer-heavy tasks).\n  - Headache location: frontal/around the head, not clearly unilateral; associated nausea. Headache episodes are variable in timing.\n  - Prodrome/associated symptoms: feeling hot, occasional shortness of breath (even at desk), heart beating fast (tachycardia), tingling in both arms, reduced appetite, unintentional weight loss (~5 lb). Libido decreased. Stress level high (7–8/10).\n  - Triggers: work stress and likely prolonged computer use; morning elevator episodes described.\n* Past medical history: Known hyperthyroidism.\n* Medications/allergies: Takes thyroxine (levothyroxine); adherent to medication; no reported drug allergies.\n* Family/social history: Works as a lawyer in London; high occupational stress; drinks alcohol weekly (a couple of glasses of wine); does not smoke; unclear family history.\n* Vital signs/exam findings: No vital signs or physical examination findings provided in transcript; plan suggested for clinic exam (heart and lungs) and blood tests.\n* Labs/imaging (mentioned): Plan to obtain blood tests including thyroxine (T4) levels and other basic labs; no imaging results provided.\n* Summary impression: A cluster of symptoms—headache with nausea, heat intolerance, tachycardia, weight loss, decreased appetite, tremor/paresthesias (tingling), reduced libido—occurring in the setting of known hyperthyroidism and recent job change, with substantial stress. This raises suspicion for thyrotoxicosis/hyperthyroid symptoms, but alternative explanations (headache disorders, anxiety) should be considered and evaluated with targeted history and investigations.",
